comments[8]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-08T05:48:45.518Z","harness:claude-code","Release impact (2026-11-08): the deliberate sandbox reproduction of this defect during the PR 1685 review ran with telemetry enabled. Its 4 events (02:37Z, environment automation, commands comments and get) became Sentry PM-CLI-3Y, an unexpected_fault TypeError, and the sentry-telemetry-gate blocked the 03:45Z morning-dispatcher Auto Release (blocker GitHub issue 1686). PM-CLI-3Y was annotated with the cause and resolved, the gate passed locally, and closing 1686 fired the blocker retry. pm get crashes on the same document too, not only pm comments. Lesson for reproductions: set PM_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1 when deliberately triggering a crash, because a sandboxed PM_PATH does not suppress delivery.\n"
  "2026-11-08T08:03:22.635Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review of PR 1687 (P2, fixed): once malformed entries load, buildSearchCorpus in src/core/search/corpus.ts still mapped every comments/notes/learnings entry to entry.text. A null entry threw a TypeError in the BM25/semantic corpus and in the reindex corpus, aborting search for every item, where main had skipped the damaged item with a warning. annotationTexts now keeps only plain-object entries with string text. The spec adds a corpus case, and that case fails against the previous corpus.ts. A built-CLI sweep over list, search, get, context, comments, notes, learnings, validate, stats, health, activity and graph impact showed the damaged item and its healthy neighbour both delivered with exit 0.\n"
  "2026-11-08T08:20:57.470Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 of PR 1687 (P2, fixed). A stored comments[2]: null,null loaded, but --if-absent then crashed. appendAnnotationEntry dereferenced entry.author in its duplicate predicate, on the CLI, MCP and SDK ifAbsent paths for all three collections. The same review surfaced a second case: an edit of a malformed entry spread a bare string into character keys (0, 1, 2 and so on).\n\nThe fix routes both through isAnnotationRecord. The duplicate check skips non-record entries. editedAnnotationEntry replaces a malformed entry with a well-formed collection entry via the collection's createEntry, so an edit repairs it. The spec covers an idempotent append twice plus a repair-by-edit for comments, notes and learnings, and the three cases fail against the previous annotations.ts.\n"
  "2026-11-08T08:41:12.432Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit review of PR 1687, two minor findings, both fixed.\n\n(1) Comparator cycle. compareTimestampStrings compares two parseable stamps by time and a mixed pair as text, so mixed valid and invalid created_at values could form a cycle. Example: A 2026-11-08T00:30:00+01:00 sorts before B 2026-11-07T23:45:00Z by time, B before X 2026-11-07T99 as text, and X before A as text. compareLogValues now groups by hasParseableTimestamp first (unparseable stamps sort first), then compares within each group. All six orderings of the three entries now sort the same way.\n\n(2) Blank refusal text. The guarded-edit refusal printed a blank time for created_at \"\" and the raw number for created_at 42. It now labels both as an unrecorded time, and details.actual_created_at reports a stored string or null.\n\nThe spec adds both cases, and they fail against the previous code.\n"
  "2026-11-08T08:54:00.137Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 of PR 1687 (P2, fixed): the canonical annotation order is hashed into item history (canonicalDocument feeds history hashing and replay for every existing hash epoch). The grouping added for the CodeRabbit cycle finding reordered documents the legacy comparator could already sort, for example an ISO stamp next to \"zzzz\". An unchanged stream written before the change then reported verify_failed:after_hash_mismatch:entry_1. The first cut of this fix carried the same risk in a quieter form: it coerced a missing field to \"\", where the legacy localeCompare argument had read \"undefined\". Two entries sharing created_at, one without text, could therefore flip.\n\nThe comparator is now legacy-exact. logSortFields coerces with String(), as the legacy Date.parse and localeCompare arguments were, and the grouping is reverted. Every document the legacy comparator could sort keeps its order and hashes, and null or primitive entries sort instead of throwing. A pairwise spec compares against an inline copy of the legacy comparator wherever that copy does not throw. It fails against both the grouping comparator and the empty-string version. The transitive order for mixed stamps needs a versioned canonicalization transition, filed as pm-yo46im.\n"
  "2026-11-08T09:06:59.097Z","harness:claude-code","Greptile review of PR 1687 (P1, fixed): the legacy-exact logSortFields converted every field with String() up front. A stored field holding valid JSON such as text: {\"toString\": 42} makes String() throw \"Cannot convert object to primitive value\". The legacy comparator only converted text when created_at tied, so the item used to load and now failed. logSortKeyString now falls back to the Object.prototype.toString tag when conversion throws. Where String() succeeds it is the same ToString the legacy comparator applied, so legacy equivalence holds. The pairwise spec adds that value as a text and as a created_at and asserts that no pair throws; that check fails against the previous commit.\n"
  "2026-11-08T09:10:37.683Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 5 of PR 1687 (P2) independently reproduced the Greptile finding at fcc3f415d. Through both the TOON and JSON Markdown parsers, an item with text: {\"toString\": null} on an entry with a distinct created_at loaded on main and threw at fcc3f415d. The same logSortKeyString fix covers it. The added full-document spec parses that item in both formats, including an author array that contains the unconvertible object, and fails against fcc3f415d. Codex also ran 2,000 random sorts (1,898,201 comparator calls) over coercible values; all of them matched the legacy order, and it found no other defect in the SDK/search handling, the release wiring or the docs.\n"
  "2026-11-08T09:23:27.358Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 6 of PR 1687, two P2 findings reachable through public SDK callers passing exotic objects, both fixed.\n\n(1) Eager reads. Converting all three fields up front read and converted text and author where the legacy comparator never did. A throwing text getter, a toString that never returns, or a conversion that mutates another entry could therefore abort, hang or reverse a sort. 5,000-deep array texts also cost about 3.4 s on distinct-timestamp items. compareLogValues now reads created_at first, text only on a created_at tie and author only on a text tie, in the legacy order.\n\n(2) Fallback could also throw. The fallback invoked Object.prototype.toString, and with it a Symbol.toStringTag getter, which can itself throw. logSortField now returns the fixed key [unconvertible] when a read or conversion throws.\n\nThe spec proves the text getter is never read for distinct stamps, and that a hostile tag getter sorts without throwing. Both fail against 3b9ef71cf.\n"
